      "text": "Gertrude Caroline Ederle was born on October 23, 1905, in New York City, United States.",
      "date": "1905-10-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Olympic Gold Medalist",
      "text": "At the 1924 Summer Olympics in Paris, Gertrude Ederle won a gold medal as part of the U.S. 4×100-meter freestyle relay team, which set a world record. She also won bronze medals in the 100-meter and 400-meter freestyle events.",
      "date": "1924"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Woman to Swim Across the English Channel",
      "text": "On August 6, 1926, Gertrude Ederle became the first woman to swim across the English Channel. She completed the swim in 14 hours and 34 minutes, breaking the previous men's record by nearly two hours.",
      "date": "1926-08-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retirement from Competitive Swimming",
      "text": "After her historic swim across the English Channel, Gertrude Ederle retired from competitive swimming and later taught swimming to deaf children.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Gertrude Ederle",
      "text": "Gertrude Ederle passed away on November 30, 2003, in Wyckoff, New Jersey, United States.",
      "date": "2003-11-30"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Animal Farm",
      "text": "Animal Farm, a satirical allegorical novella by George Orwell, was first published in England on 17 August 1945.",
      "date": "1945-08-17"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Rebellion",
      "text": "The animals on the farm, led by the pigs Snowball and Napoleon, rebel against their human farmer, Mr. Jones, hoping to create a society where animals are equal, free, and happy.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Establishment of Animalism",
      "text": "Following the rebellion, the animals establish a new set of principles called Animalism, which promotes equality and freedom for all animals.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of the Cowshed",
      "text": "The animals successfully defend the farm from an attempted recapture by Mr. Jones and other humans in an event known as the Battle of the Cowshed.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Expulsion of Snowball",
      "text": "Napoleon, one of the leading pigs, expels Snowball from the farm, consolidating his power and beginning his dictatorship.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction of the Windmill",
      "text": "Under Napoleon's rule, the animals begin the construction of a windmill, which becomes a central project on the farm.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Betrayal of the Rebellion",
      "text": "The ideals of the rebellion are betrayed as Napoleon becomes increasingly tyrannical, and the farm ends up in a state far worse than before.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transformation of Pigs",
      "text": "By the end of the story, the pigs have become indistinguishable from humans, symbolizing the complete corruption of the original ideals.",
      "date": "1945"
    }
  ]
}

