      "headline": "First Description of Autism",
      "text": "In 1943, Leo Kanner, an Austrian-American psychiatrist, first described autism in a group of 11 children with similar symptoms, which he termed 'early infantile autism'.",
      "date": "1943"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Hans Asperger's Contribution",
      "text": "In 1944, Hans Asperger, an Austrian pediatrician, described a milder form of autism, later known as Asperger syndrome, in children who had difficulties in social interaction but normal language and cognitive development.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Autism in DSM-III",
      "text": "In 1980, autism was included as a distinct diagnosis in the third edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-III) by the American Psychiatric Association.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Asperger Syndrome in DSM-IV",
      "text": "In 1994, Asperger syndrome was added as a separate diagnosis in the fourth edition of the DSM (DSM-IV).",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Autism Spectrum Disorder in DSM-5",
      "text": "In 2013, the DSM-5 was published, which consolidated all autism-related disorders, including Asperger syndrome, into a single diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD).",
      "date": "2013"
    }
  ]
}

