      "headline": "Classical Sanskrit Standardization",
      "text": "Panini, an ancient Indian scholar, standardized Classical Sanskrit with his work 'Ashtadhyayi', a comprehensive grammar of Sanskrit, around the 4th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0400"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sanskrit as a Sacred Language",
      "text": "Sanskrit became the sacred language of Hinduism and the language of classical Hindu philosophy, as well as historical texts of Buddhism and Jainism.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sanskrit in Ancient and Medieval South Asia",
      "text": "Sanskrit served as a link language in ancient and medieval South Asia, facilitating communication and cultural exchange.",
      "date": "0000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transmission to Southeast Asia, East Asia, and Central Asia",
      "text": "During the early medieval era, Sanskrit was transmitted along with Hindu and Buddhist culture to Southeast Asia, East Asia, and Central Asia, becoming a language of religion and high culture.",
      "date": "0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Influence on Regional Languages",
      "text": "Sanskrit had a lasting effect on the languages of South Asia, Southeast Asia, and East Asia, especially in their formal and learned vocabularies.",
