      "headline": "First Use of Iron Lung",
      "text": "The first iron lung was used in 1928 at the Boston Children's Hospital. It was developed by Philip Drinker and Louis Agassiz Shaw, professors of industrial hygiene at Harvard University.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commercial Production Begins",
      "text": "In 1929, the iron lung was commercialized by the company Warren E. Collins, Inc., making it more widely available for hospitals.",
      "date": "1929"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Improvement by John Haven Emerson",
      "text": "In 1931, John Haven Emerson made improvements to the original design, making the iron lung more efficient and easier to use.",
      "date": "1931"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Polio Epidemic Increases Demand",
      "text": "During the 1940s and 1950s, the polio epidemic led to a significant increase in the use of iron lungs, as they were essential for patients with respiratory paralysis.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline in Use",
      "text": "The introduction of the polio vaccine in the mid-1950s led to a dramatic decline in the number of polio cases and, consequently, the need for iron lungs.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern Alternatives",
      "text": "By the late 20th century, more advanced and less cumbersome ventilators had largely replaced the iron lung, although a few patients continued to use them into the 21st century.",
      "date": "1980"
    }
  ]
}

