      "headline": "Foundation of Sinn Féin",
      "text": "Sinn Féin was founded on November 28, 1905, by Arthur Griffith in Dublin, Ireland, as a political movement to establish an independent Irish Republic.",
      "date": "1905-11-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1916 Easter Rising",
      "text": "During the Easter Rising of April 1916, Sinn Féin was mistakenly associated with the rebellion, which increased its popularity and membership.",
      "date": "1916-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1918 General Election Success",
      "text": "In the 1918 general election, Sinn Féin won 73 out of 105 Irish seats, leading to the establishment of the First Dáil in January 1919.",
      "date": "1918"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Anglo-Irish Treaty and Split",
      "text": "The signing of the Anglo-Irish Treaty in December 1921 led to a split in Sinn Féin, with pro-Treaty members forming Cumann na nGaedheal and anti-Treaty members continuing as Sinn Féin.",
      "date": "1921-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of Fianna Fáil",
      "text": "In 1926, Éamon de Valera and his supporters left Sinn Féin to form Fianna Fáil, which would become a dominant political party in Ireland.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sinn Féin and the IRA",
      "text": "Throughout the 20th century, Sinn Féin maintained a close relationship with the Irish Republican Army (IRA), supporting its campaign for a united Ireland.",
      "date": "1905-2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Good Friday Agreement",
      "text": "Sinn Féin played a key role in the negotiations leading to the Good Friday Agreement, signed on April 10, 1998, which helped to bring peace to Northern Ireland.",
      "date": "1998-04-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sinn Féin in Government",
      "text": "In 2007, Sinn Féin entered the Northern Ireland Executive, sharing power with the Democratic Unionist Party (DUP).",
      "date": "2007"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Historic Election Success",
      "text": "In the 2020 general election, Sinn Féin won the popular vote in the Republic of Ireland for the first time, securing 37 seats in the Dáil.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

