      "headline": "First Trip to Africa",
      "text": "In 1957, Jane Goodall traveled to Kenya, marking her first trip to Africa. This journey would eventually lead to her groundbreaking work with chimpanzees.",
      "date": "1957"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Meeting Louis Leakey",
      "text": "In 1958, Jane Goodall met the renowned anthropologist and paleontologist Louis Leakey, who would become her mentor and help her begin her research on chimpanzees.",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrival at Gombe Stream National Park",
      "text": "In July 1960, Jane Goodall arrived at Gombe Stream National Park in Tanzania to begin her study of the social and family interactions of wild chimpanzees.",
      "date": "1960-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Tool Use in Chimpanzees",
      "text": "In 1960, Jane Goodall made the groundbreaking discovery that chimpanzees make and use tools, a trait previously thought to be unique to humans.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Marriage to Hugo van Lawick",
      "text": "Jane Goodall married Dutch wildlife photographer Hugo van Lawick on March 28, 1964.",
      "date": "1964-03-28"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Birth of Son",
      "text": "Jane Goodall gave birth to her son, Hugo Eric Louis van Lawick, affectionately known as 'Grub', on March 4, 1967.",
      "date": "1967-03-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of the Jane Goodall Institute",
      "text": "In 1977, Jane Goodall founded the Jane Goodall Institute, which supports the research at Gombe and is a global leader in the effort to protect chimpanzees and their habitats.",
      "date": "1977"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roots & Shoots Program Launch",
      "text": "In 1991, Jane Goodall launched the Roots & Shoots program, a global youth-led community action program.",
