      "headline": "Martin Luther's 95 Theses",
      "text": "On October 31, 1517, Martin Luther nailed his 95 Theses to the door of the Wittenberg Castle church, challenging the Catholic Church's practices, particularly the sale of indulgences.",
      "date": "1517-10-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Diet of Worms",
      "text": "In 1521, Martin Luther was summoned to the Diet of Worms, where he was asked to recant his teachings. He refused, leading to his excommunication and the Edict of Worms, which declared him an outlaw.",
      "date": "1521"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Lutheran Church",
      "text": "By the mid-1520s, the Lutheran Church began to take shape as a distinct branch of Christianity, following Martin Luther's teachings and reforms.",
      "date": "1520s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Augsburg Confession",
      "text": "In 1530, the Augsburg Confession was presented at the Diet of Augsburg. It is one of the most important documents of Lutheranism, outlining the faith's core beliefs.",
      "date": "1530"
    },
    {
      "headline": "John Calvin's Institutes of the Christian Religion",
      "text": "In 1536, John Calvin published 'Institutes of the Christian Religion,' a foundational work for Reformed theology and a major influence on Protestantism.",
      "date": "1536"
    },
    {
      "headline": "English Reformation",
      "text": "The English Reformation began in 1534 when King Henry VIII broke away from the Roman Catholic Church and established the Church of England.",
      "date": "1534"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peace of Augsburg",
      "text": "In 1555, the Peace of Augsburg was signed, allowing for the coexistence of Catholicism and Lutheranism in the Holy Roman Empire, and granting rulers the right to choose the religion of their own state.",
      "date": "1555"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Thirty Years' War",
      "text": "From 1618 to 1648, the Thirty Years' War, a major conflict across Europe, was partly fueled by religious tensions between Protestant and Catholic states.",
      "date": "1618"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peace of Westphalia",
      "text": "In 1648, the Peace of Westphalia ended the Thirty Years' War and marked a turning point in European history, recognizing the sovereignty of Protestant states.",
      "date": "1648"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Awakening",
      "text": "In the 18th century, the Great Awakening was a series of religious revivals in the American colonies, emphasizing Protestant evangelicalism.",
      "date": "18th century"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Vatican Council",
      "text": "From 1962 to 1965, the Second Vatican Council addressed relations between the Roman Catholic Church and the modern world, including its approach to Protestantism.",
      "date": "1962"
    }
  ]
}

{
