      "headline": "Old Persian Language",
      "text": "Old Persian was used in the Achaemenid Empire, with the earliest known inscriptions dating back to the reign of Darius I (522-486 BCE).",
      "date": "-0522"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Middle Persian Language",
      "text": "Middle Persian, also known as Pahlavi, was used during the Sassanian Empire (224-651 CE) and is the direct ancestor of Modern Persian.",
      "date": "0224"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Emergence of New Persian",
      "text": "New Persian, the direct ancestor of today's Persian language, began to emerge around the 9th century CE, following the Islamic conquest of Persia.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of the Persian Alphabet",
      "text": "The Persian alphabet, a derivative of the Arabic script, was adopted for writing Persian during the Islamic Golden Age, around the 9th to 10th centuries CE.",
      "date": "0900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Persian as a Literary Language",
      "text": "By the 10th century, Persian became a prominent literary language in the Islamic world, with poets like Ferdowsi and Rumi contributing to its rich literary tradition.",
      "date": "1000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Persian in the Mughal Empire",
      "text": "During the Mughal Empire (1526-1857), Persian was the official language of administration and culture in the Indian subcontinent.",
      "date": "1526"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Tajik Alphabet",
      "text": "In the 20th century, the Tajik variety of Persian began to be written in the Tajik alphabet, a derivative of the Cyrillic script, in Tajikistan.",
      "date": "1928"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Persian as an Official Language",
      "text": "Persian is recognized as an official language in Iran, Afghanistan (as Dari), and Tajikistan (as Tajiki), with each country having its own standard variety.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

