      "text": "Around 3,500 years ago, the Lapita people discovered and settled the Samoan Islands, marking the beginning of the Samoan cultural identity.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "European Contact",
      "text": "In 1722, Dutch explorer Jacob Roggeveen became the first European to sight the Samoan Islands.",
      "date": "1722"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Missionaries Arrive",
      "text": "In 1830, John Williams of the London Missionary Society arrived in Samoa, marking the beginning of significant European influence.",
      "date": "1830"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tripartite Convention",
      "text": "In 1899, the Tripartite Convention was signed, dividing the Samoan Islands between Germany and the United States, with Germany taking control of Western Samoa.",
      "date": "1899"
    },
    {
      "headline": "German Samoa",
      "text": "From 1900 to 1914, Samoa was a German colony known as German Samoa.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "New Zealand Occupation",
      "text": "In 1914, New Zealand troops occupied German Samoa at the start of World War I.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "League of Nations Mandate",
      "text": "In 1920, the League of Nations granted New Zealand a mandate to administer Western Samoa.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mau Movement",
      "text": "In the 1920s and 1930s, the Mau movement, a non-violent resistance movement, gained momentum in Samoa, advocating for independence from colonial rule.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Independence",
      "text": "On January 1, 1962, Western Samoa became the first Pacific island country to gain independence.",
      "date": "1962-01-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Name Change to Samoa",
      "text": "In 1997, Western Samoa officially changed its name to Samoa.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Time Zone Change",
      "text": "On December 29, 2011, Samoa moved west of the International Date Line, skipping December 30, to align its time zone with its main trading partners.",
      "date": "2011-12-29"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Judgment of Paris",
      "text": "The events leading to the Trojan War began with the Judgment of Paris, where Paris, prince of Troy, was asked to choose the fairest goddess between Hera, Athena, and Aphrodite. He chose Aphrodite, who promised him the most beautiful woman in the world, Helen of Sparta.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Abduction of Helen",
      "text": "Paris abducted Helen, the wife of Menelaus, king of Sparta, leading to the Greeks organizing a large expedition to retrieve her, marking the beginning of the Trojan War.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Greek Expedition to Troy",
      "text": "The Greek forces, led by Agamemnon, brother of Menelaus, set sail for Troy to retrieve Helen and punish the Trojans.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Siege of Troy Begins",
      "text": "The Greeks laid siege to the city of Troy, a conflict that would last for ten years.",
      "date": "-0120"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Achilles Withdraws from Battle",
      "text": "During the tenth year of the siege, Achilles, the Greeks' greatest warrior, withdrew from battle due to a dispute with Agamemnon over the captive Briseis.",
      "date": "-0110"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Patroclus",
      "text": "Achilles' close friend Patroclus was killed by Hector, the Trojan prince, while wearing Achilles' armor.",
      "date": "-0110"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Achilles Kills Hector",
      "text": "In revenge for Patroclus' death, Achilles killed Hector and dragged his body around the walls of Troy.",
      "date": "-0110"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trojan Horse and Fall of Troy",
      "text": "The Greeks used the stratagem of the Trojan Horse to enter and sack the city of Troy, leading to its fall.",
      "date": "-0110"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return of the Greek Heroes",
      "text": "After the fall of Troy, the Greek heroes, including Odysseus, began their journeys home, which were fraught with challenges and adventures.",
      "date": "-0110"
    }
  ]
}

