      "text": "The first 24 Hours of Le Mans race was held on May 26-27, 1923. It was organized by the Automobile Club de l'Ouest (ACO) and took place on the Circuit de la Sarthe, near Le Mans, France.",
      "date": "1923-05-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Major Tragedy",
      "text": "In 1955, the 24 Hours of Le Mans was the site of a major disaster when a crash killed 83 spectators and driver Pierre Levegh. This incident led to significant changes in safety regulations in motorsport.",
      "date": "1955"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ford's Dominance Begins",
      "text": "In 1966, Ford achieved its first victory at the 24 Hours of Le Mans with the Ford GT40, marking the beginning of a four-year winning streak for the American manufacturer.",
      "date": "1966"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Porsche's Record",
      "text": "In 1983, Porsche set a record with its ninth overall win at Le Mans, surpassing Ferrari's previous record of eight wins.",
      "date": "1983"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Audi's Era of Success",
      "text": "Audi began its dominance at Le Mans in 2000, winning the race for the first time. This marked the start of a period where Audi would win 13 times in 15 years.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Toyota's Historic Win",
      "text": "In 2018, Toyota won the 24 Hours of Le Mans for the first time, with their TS050 Hybrid, after years of near misses and heartbreaks.",
      "date": "2018"
    }
  ]
}

