      "headline": "Introduction of Neoliberalism",
      "text": "Neoliberalism emerged as a distinct political philosophy in the mid-20th century, advocating for free-market capitalism, deregulation, and reduction in government spending.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mont Pelerin Society Founded",
      "text": "In 1947, the Mont Pelerin Society was founded by Friedrich Hayek and other intellectuals to promote neoliberal ideas and counteract collectivism.",
      "date": "1947"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Milton Friedman's Influence",
      "text": "In the 1950s and 1960s, economist Milton Friedman became a leading advocate for neoliberal policies, emphasizing monetarism and free-market principles.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chicago School of Economics",
      "text": "During the 1960s and 1970s, the Chicago School of Economics, led by figures like Milton Friedman, became a major center for neoliberal thought.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pinochet's Economic Reforms in Chile",
      "text": "In 1973, after the military coup in Chile, Augusto Pinochet implemented neoliberal economic reforms advised by the 'Chicago Boys,' a group of Chilean economists trained at the University of Chicago.",
      "date": "1973"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Margaret Thatcher's Premiership",
      "text": "Margaret Thatcher became Prime Minister of the United Kingdom in 1979, implementing neoliberal policies such as privatization, deregulation, and reducing the power of trade unions.",
      "date": "1979"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ronald Reagan's Presidency",
      "text": "Ronald Reagan was elected President of the United States in 1980, promoting neoliberal economic policies including tax cuts, deregulation, and a reduction in government spending.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Washington Consensus",
      "text": "In 1989, the term 'Washington Consensus' was coined to describe a set of neoliberal economic policy prescriptions for developing countries, emphasizing market liberalization, fiscal discipline, and privatization.",
      "date": "1989"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Spread of Neoliberalism",
      "text": "Throughout the 1990s and 2000s, neoliberal policies spread globally, influencing economic reforms in many countries and international institutions like the IMF and World Bank.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Criticism and Backlash",
      "text": "In the 2000s and 2010s, neoliberalism faced increasing criticism for contributing to economic inequality, financial instability, and undermining social welfare systems.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

