      "headline": "Archaeological Work in the Middle East",
      "text": "From 1910 to 1914, Lawrence worked as an archaeologist at Carchemish in Syria, under the direction of D. G. Hogarth.",
      "date": "1910"
    },
    {
      "headline": "World War I Service Begins",
      "text": "In 1914, Lawrence was commissioned as a second lieutenant in the British Army and was stationed in Cairo, Egypt.",
      "date": "1914"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Role in the Arab Revolt",
      "text": "In 1916, Lawrence was sent to Arabia to assist the Arab Revolt against the Ottoman Empire, where he became a liaison to the Arab forces.",
      "date": "1916"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Capture of Aqaba",
      "text": "On July 6, 1917, Lawrence and Arab forces captured the strategic port of Aqaba from the Ottoman Empire.",
      "date": "1917-07-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of 'Seven Pillars of Wisdom'",
      "text": "In 1926, Lawrence published his account of the Arab Revolt, 'Seven Pillars of Wisdom', which became a classic of war literature.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Enlistment in the Royal Air Force",
      "text": "Lawrence enlisted in the Royal Air Force under the name John Hume Ross in 1922, but was discharged in 1923 when his identity was discovered.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Re-enlistment in the Royal Air Force",
      "text": "In 1925, Lawrence re-enlisted in the RAF under the name T. E. Shaw and served until 1935.",
      "date": "1925"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of T. E. Lawrence",
      "text": "Lawrence died on May 19, 1935, from injuries sustained in a motorcycle accident near his home in Dorset, England.",
      "date": "1935-05-19"
    }
  ]
}

