    {
      "headline": "Onset of the Potato Blight",
      "text": "In September 1845, the potato blight, Phytophthora infestans, was first observed in Ireland. This marked the beginning of the Great Famine as the potato crop, a staple food for the Irish population, began to fail.",
      "date": "1845-09"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Year of Famine",
      "text": "By 1846, the potato crop failure was widespread, leading to severe food shortages. The British government's response was inadequate, and the situation worsened as the blight returned.",
      "date": "1846"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of the Famine",
      "text": "The year 1847, known as 'Black '47', saw the peak of the famine. Starvation and disease were rampant, and the death toll rose significantly.",
      "date": "1847"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Continued Hardship and Emigration",
      "text": "In 1848, the famine continued, and many Irish people began to emigrate in large numbers to escape the dire conditions. The population of Ireland began to decline sharply.",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decline of the Famine",
      "text": "By 1850, the worst of the famine was over, but the effects were still felt. The population had decreased significantly due to death and emigration.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "End of the Famine",
      "text": "The Great Famine officially ended in 1852, but its impact on Ireland was long-lasting, with significant demographic, social, and economic changes.",
      "date": "1852"
    }
  ]
}

