      "headline": "First Use of the Tricolour",
      "text": "The Irish tricolour was first used by Thomas Francis Meagher in 1848. It was intended to symbolize the inclusion and hoped-for union between Roman Catholics (represented by the green) and Protestants (represented by the orange).",
      "date": "1848"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Easter Rising",
      "text": "During the Easter Rising of 1916, the tricolour was raised above the General Post Office in Dublin by Irish republicans, marking its association with the Irish independence movement.",
      "date": "1916-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption as National Flag",
      "text": "The tricolour was formally adopted as the national flag of the Irish Free State in 1922 following the Anglo-Irish Treaty.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Constitution of Ireland",
      "text": "The Constitution of Ireland, enacted in 1937, confirmed the tricolour as the national flag of Ireland.",
      "date": "1937"
    }
  ]
}

