    {
      "headline": "Commercial Production Begins",
      "text": "In 1797, Major Dubied, along with his son-in-law Henri-Louis Pernod, opened the first absinthe distillery, Dubied Père et Fils, in Couvet, Switzerland.",
      "date": "1797"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Pernod Fils Distillery Established",
      "text": "In 1805, Henri-Louis Pernod opened the Pernod Fils distillery in Pontarlier, France, which became one of the most famous producers of absinthe.",
      "date": "1805"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Absinthe Gains Popularity",
      "text": "By the mid-19th century, absinthe had become extremely popular in France, particularly among artists and writers, and was often referred to as 'la fée verte' or 'the green fairy'.",
      "date": "1850"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Peak of Absinthe Popularity",
      "text": "Absinthe reached the height of its popularity in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, especially in France, where it became associated with bohemian culture.",
