      "headline": "Introduction of Pound Sterling",
      "text": "The pound sterling was introduced as the currency of England in 1158 under King Henry II. It was initially a silver coin known as the 'sterling'.",
      "date": "1158"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Great Recoinage",
      "text": "In 1696, the Great Recoinage was initiated to address the problem of clipped and counterfeit coins. This recoinage was a major reform of the currency system in England.",
      "date": "1696"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Gold Standard Adoption",
      "text": "The United Kingdom officially adopted the gold standard in 1821, which linked the value of the pound to a specific quantity of gold.",
      "date": "1821"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decimalisation",
      "text": "On 15 February 1971, the United Kingdom decimalised its currency, replacing the old system of pounds, shillings, and pence with a system based on 100 new pence to the pound.",
      "date": "1971-02-15"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Black Wednesday",
      "text": "On 16 September 1992, known as Black Wednesday, the UK was forced to withdraw the pound from the European Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM) after it was unable to keep the pound above its agreed lower limit.",
      "date": "1992-09-16"
    }
  ]
}

