    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Bechdel Test",
      "text": "The Bechdel Test was introduced in 1985 in Alison Bechdel's comic strip 'Dykes to Watch Out For'. The test was formulated in a strip titled 'The Rule', where a character explains that she only watches a movie if it satisfies three basic criteria: it has to have at least two women in it, who talk to each other, about something besides a man.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Popularization of the Bechdel Test",
      "text": "Over the years, the Bechdel Test gained popularity as a tool to highlight gender inequality in fiction, particularly in films. It became a standard measure for evaluating the representation of women in media.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bechdel Test in Academic and Cultural Discussions",
      "text": "By the 2000s and 2010s, the Bechdel Test was widely discussed in academic circles and cultural critiques as a simple yet effective measure of gender bias in media.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bechdel Test Database",
      "text": "In 2013, a database was created to track films that pass or fail the Bechdel Test, providing a resource for those interested in gender representation in movies.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Bechdel Test's Influence on Film Industry",
      "text": "The Bechdel Test has influenced filmmakers and audiences to be more aware of gender representation, leading to increased scrutiny and discussions about the roles of women in film.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

