      "headline": "Oklahoma City Bombing",
      "text": "On April 19, 1995, the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma, was bombed in a domestic terrorist attack. The bombing resulted in the deaths of 168 people and injured over 600 others. It remains the deadliest act of domestic terrorism in U.S. history.",
      "date": "1995-04-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest of Timothy McVeigh",
      "text": "Timothy McVeigh was arrested on April 19, 1995, shortly after the bombing, for driving without a license plate and illegal possession of a firearm. He was later identified as the primary suspect in the bombing.",
      "date": "1995-04-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Arrest of Terry Nichols",
      "text": "Terry Nichols, an accomplice in the bombing, was arrested on April 21, 1995. He was charged with conspiracy and eight counts of involuntary manslaughter.",
      "date": "1995-04-21"
    },
    {
      "headline": "McVeigh's Conviction",
      "text": "On June 2, 1997, Timothy McVeigh was found guilty on 11 counts of murder and conspiracy in connection with the Oklahoma City bombing.",
      "date": "1997-06-02"
    },
    {
      "headline": "McVeigh's Execution",
      "text": "Timothy McVeigh was executed by lethal injection on June 11, 2001, at the Federal Correctional Complex in Terre Haute, Indiana.",
      "date": "2001-06-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nichols' Conviction",
      "text": "Terry Nichols was convicted on December 24, 1997, of conspiracy to use a weapon of mass destruction and eight counts of involuntary manslaughter.",
      "date": "1997-12-24"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nichols' Life Sentence",
      "text": "On June 4, 1998, Terry Nichols was sentenced to life in prison without the possibility of parole for his role in the bombing.",
      "date": "1998-06-04"
