      "headline": "Establishment of Vichy France",
      "text": "On July 10, 1940, the French National Assembly granted full powers to Marshal Philippe Pétain, effectively establishing the Vichy regime. This followed the armistice signed with Nazi Germany on June 22, 1940, which led to the occupation of northern and western France.",
      "date": "1940-07-10"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vichy Government Relocates to Vichy",
      "text": "The Vichy government, officially known as the French State, established its seat in the city of Vichy, located in the unoccupied 'free zone' of France.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Vichy France's Collaboration Policy",
      "text": "Throughout its existence, Vichy France adopted a policy of collaboration with Nazi Germany, which included cooperation in the deportation of Jews and other repressive measures.",
      "date": "1940"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Operation Torch and German Occupation of Southern France",
      "text": "In response to the Allied landings in North Africa (Operation Torch) on November 8, 1942, German and Italian forces occupied the remainder of Metropolitan France, ending any pretense of independence by the Vichy government.",
      "date": "1942-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Liberation of France and End of Vichy Regime",
      "text": "The Vichy regime effectively ended with the Allied liberation of France in 1944. The Provisional Government of the French Republic, led by Charles de Gaulle, took over the administration of France.",
      "date": "1944"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Trial and Conviction of Philippe Pétain",
      "text": "After the war, Philippe Pétain was tried and convicted for treason. He was sentenced to death in 1945, but his sentence was commuted to life imprisonment.",
      "date": "1945"
    }
  ]
}

