      "headline": "First Use of 'Black Friday'",
      "text": "The term 'Black Friday' was first used in the 1950s by police in Philadelphia to describe the chaos that ensued on the day after Thanksgiving, when hordes of suburban shoppers and tourists flooded into the city in advance of the Army-Navy football game held on that Saturday every year. Not only would the police not be able to take the day off, but they would have to work extra-long shifts dealing with the additional crowds and traffic.",
      "date": "1950"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retailers Adopt 'Black Friday'",
      "text": "In the 1980s, retailers began to adopt the term 'Black Friday' to refer to the point in the year when retailers begin to turn a profit, or are 'in the black'.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Black Friday Becomes the Busiest Shopping Day",
      "text": "By the late 1990s, Black Friday had become the busiest shopping day of the year in the United States, as retailers began to open earlier and offer significant sales and discounts.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cyber Monday Introduction",
      "text": "In 2005, the term 'Cyber Monday' was coined by Ellen Davis and Scott Silverman to encourage people to shop online on the Monday following Black Friday.",
      "date": "2005"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Thanksgiving Day Openings",
      "text": "In the 2010s, many retailers began opening their doors on Thanksgiving Day itself, starting their Black Friday sales earlier than ever.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "COVID-19 Pandemic Impact",
      "text": "In 2020, due to the COVID-19 pandemic, many retailers extended their Black Friday sales throughout November to reduce crowding and encourage online shopping.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

