      "headline": "Pre-Colonial Era",
      "text": "Before the arrival of European colonizers, the region that is now Niger was home to several powerful empires and kingdoms, including the Songhai Empire, the Mali Empire, and the Kanem-Bornu Empire.",
      "date": "-1500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "French Colonial Rule",
      "text": "In the late 19th century, Niger became part of French West Africa. It was officially established as a French colony in 1922.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Independence from France",
      "text": "Niger gained independence from France on August 3, 1960.",
      "date": "1960-08-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Republic Established",
      "text": "Following independence, Niger established its First Republic with Hamani Diori as its first president.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Military Coup",
      "text": "In 1974, a military coup led by Lieutenant Colonel Seyni Kountché overthrew President Hamani Diori, marking the beginning of military rule.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Return to Civilian Rule",
      "text": "In 1993, Niger transitioned back to civilian rule with the election of Mahamane Ousmane as president.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Second Military Coup",
      "text": "In 1996, another military coup led by Ibrahim Baré Maïnassara ousted the civilian government.",
      "date": "1996"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Third Military Coup",
      "text": "In 1999, a coup led by Daouda Malam Wanké resulted in the assassination of President Maïnassara and the establishment of a transitional government.",
      "date": "1999"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fourth Republic",
      "text": "In 1999, Niger adopted a new constitution and held elections, leading to the establishment of the Fourth Republic with Mamadou Tandja as president.",
      "date": "1999"
