      "headline": "Initial Proposal for Christ the Redeemer",
      "text": "In 1920, the idea for a large statue of Christ was proposed by the Catholic Circle of Rio. The initial design was a large Christ with a globe in one hand and a cross in the other.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Design Change",
      "text": "The design of Christ the Redeemer was changed from the original proposal to the current design with arms spread wide. This change was made after the project organizers accepted the initial design but later decided on a different approach.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Construction Begins",
      "text": "Construction of Christ the Redeemer began in 1922. The statue was designed by French-Polish sculptor Paul Landowski and built by Brazilian engineer Heitor da Silva Costa, with collaboration from French engineer Albert Caquot.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sculpting of the Face",
      "text": "Romanian sculptor Gheorghe Leonida sculpted the face of Christ the Redeemer, contributing to the statue's iconic appearance.",
      "date": "1922"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of Christ the Redeemer",
      "text": "Christ the Redeemer was completed in 1931. The statue stands 30 metres (98 ft) high, excluding its 8-metre (26 ft) pedestal, with arms stretching 28 metres (92 ft) wide. It is made of reinforced concrete and soapstone.",
      "date": "1931"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Inauguration of Christ the Redeemer",
      "text": "The statue was officially inaugurated on October 12, 1931, and has since become a symbol of Christianity across the world, as well as a cultural icon of both Rio de Janeiro and Brazil.",
      "date": "1931-10-12"
    }
  ]
}

