      "text": "Jalāl al-Dīn Muḥammad Rūmī was born on September 30, 1207, in Balkh, which is now in present-day Afghanistan.",
      "date": "1207-09-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Migration to Anatolia",
      "text": "In 1228, Rumi's family migrated to Anatolia, which was then part of the Seljuk Empire, due to the Mongol invasions.",
      "date": "1228"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rumi's Father Dies",
      "text": "In 1231, Rumi's father, Baha' ud-Din Walad, passed away. Rumi succeeded him as a prominent religious teacher.",
      "date": "1231"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Meeting Shams of Tabriz",
      "text": "In 1244, Rumi met the dervish Shams of Tabriz, who became his spiritual mentor and inspired much of his poetry.",
      "date": "1244"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Disappearance of Shams",
      "text": "In 1248, Shams of Tabriz disappeared, which deeply affected Rumi and led him to compose many of his famous works.",
      "date": "1248"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Completion of the Masnavi",
      "text": "Rumi completed his magnum opus, the Masnavi, a six-volume poem, around 1273. It is considered one of the greatest works of mystical poetry.",
      "date": "1273"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Rumi",
      "text": "Rumi passed away on December 17, 1273, in Konya, which is in present-day Turkey. His death is commemorated annually as Shab-e Arus (The Night of Union).",
      "date": "1273-12-17"
    }
  ]
}

