      "headline": "Discovery of Umami",
      "text": "In 1908, Kikunae Ikeda, a Japanese chemist, discovered the umami taste while studying the flavors in kombu, a type of edible seaweed. He identified glutamate as the source of this savory taste.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Isolation of Monosodium Glutamate",
      "text": "In 1908, Kikunae Ikeda successfully isolated monosodium glutamate (MSG) from kombu and patented the process for its production.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Commercial Production Begins",
      "text": "In 1909, the commercial production of MSG began under the brand name 'Ajinomoto', which means 'essence of taste' in Japanese.",
      "date": "1909"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction to the United States",
      "text": "MSG was introduced to the United States in the 1930s, where it began to be used in various food products.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Classification",
      "text": "In 1958, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration classified MSG as 'generally recognized as safe' (GRAS).",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Chinese Restaurant Syndrome",
      "text": "In 1968, the term 'Chinese Restaurant Syndrome' was coined after a letter published in the New England Journal of Medicine described symptoms allegedly caused by MSG consumption.",
      "date": "1968"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Scientific Studies on MSG",
      "text": "Throughout the 1980s and 1990s, numerous scientific studies were conducted to investigate the safety and effects of MSG, with most finding it safe for consumption in normal quantities.",
      "date": "1980"
    },
    {
      "headline": "FDA Reaffirms Safety",
      "text": "In 1995, the FDA reaffirmed the safety of MSG, stating that it is safe for the general population.",
      "date": "1995"
    }
  ]
}

