      "headline": "Creation of Esperanto",
      "text": "Esperanto was created by L. L. Zamenhof in 1887. It was intended to be a universal second language for international communication.",
      "date": "1887"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of Dr. Esperanto's International Language",
      "text": "In 1887, L. L. Zamenhof published the first book describing Esperanto, titled 'Dr. Esperanto's International Language', under the pseudonym Doktoro Esperanto.",
      "date": "1887"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Esperanto Congress",
      "text": "The first World Esperanto Congress was held in Boulogne-sur-Mer, France, in 1905, marking a significant milestone in the language's adoption.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of the Fundamento de Esperanto",
      "text": "In 1905, L. L. Zamenhof published the 'Fundamento de Esperanto', which established the basic principles of the language.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Universal Esperanto Association",
      "text": "The Universal Esperanto Association (UEA) was founded in 1908 to promote the use of Esperanto worldwide.",
      "date": "1908"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Esperanto and the League of Nations",
      "text": "In the 1920s, there were efforts to have Esperanto adopted as a working language of the League of Nations, but it was ultimately unsuccessful.",
      "date": "1920"
    },
    {
      "headline": "UNESCO Resolution",
      "text": "In 1954, UNESCO passed a resolution recognizing the contributions of Esperanto to international understanding and cultural exchange.",
      "date": "1954"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "Creation of Photoshop",
      "text": "In 1987, Thomas and John Knoll began the development of Photoshop. Thomas Knoll, a PhD student at the University of Michigan, started writing a program on his Macintosh Plus to display grayscale images on a monochrome display. This program, called 'Display,' caught the attention of his brother John Knoll, an employee at Industrial Light & Magic, who encouraged Thomas to turn it into a full-fledged image editing program.",
      "date": "1987"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adobe's Acquisition",
      "text": "In 1988, Adobe Systems acquired the distribution license to Photoshop. The Knoll brothers had initially attempted to sell the program to other companies, but Adobe saw the potential and decided to purchase the rights.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release of Photoshop 1.0",
      "text": "Adobe Photoshop 1.0 was released on February 19, 1990, exclusively for Macintosh. This marked the beginning of Photoshop's journey as a professional image editing software.",
      "date": "1990-02-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Layers",
      "text": "In 1994, Adobe Photoshop 3.0 introduced the concept of 'layers,' which revolutionized the way images were edited and manipulated, allowing for more complex and flexible editing.",
      "date": "1994"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Photoshop 5.0 and the History Palette",
      "text": "Released in 1998, Photoshop 5.0 introduced the History Palette, which allowed users to undo multiple actions, enhancing the editing process significantly.",
      "date": "1998"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Photoshop CS and the Creative Suite",
      "text": "In 2003, Adobe released Photoshop CS (Creative Suite), which was part of a larger suite of Adobe products. This version integrated better with other Adobe applications and introduced features like the Camera Raw 2.x plugin.",
      "date": "2003"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transition to Creative Cloud",
      "text": "In 2013, Adobe transitioned Photoshop to a subscription-based model with the release of Photoshop CC (Creative Cloud). This allowed users to access the software through a monthly or annual subscription, providing regular updates and cloud storage.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of AI Features",
      "text": "In 2020, Adobe introduced AI-powered features in Photoshop, such as the Neural Filters and Sky Replacement, leveraging Adobe Sensei to enhance photo editing capabilities.",
      "date": "2020"
    }
  ]
}

