      "headline": "Introduction of the Intel 4004 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1971, Intel introduced the world's first commercially available microprocessor, the Intel 4004, which was a 4-bit central processing unit (CPU).",
      "date": "1971"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release of the Intel 8008 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1972, Intel released the 8008 microprocessor, an 8-bit CPU that was more powerful than the 4004.",
      "date": "1972"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Intel 8080 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1974, Intel introduced the 8080 microprocessor, which became the basis for the first personal computers.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Launch of the Intel 8086 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1978, Intel launched the 8086 microprocessor, which was the first 16-bit processor and laid the foundation for the x86 architecture.",
      "date": "1978"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release of the Intel 286 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1982, Intel released the 80286 microprocessor, also known as the Intel 286, which was widely used in IBM PCs.",
      "date": "1982"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Intel 386 Microprocessor",
      "text": "In 1985, Intel introduced the 386 microprocessor, a 32-bit processor that significantly improved computing power.",
      "date": "1985"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Launch of the Intel Pentium Processor",
      "text": "In 1993, Intel launched the Pentium processor, which became one of the most popular CPUs for personal computers.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Release of the Intel Core Processor",
      "text": "In 2006, Intel released the Intel Core processor line, which marked a shift towards multi-core processing.",
      "date": "2006"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Intel's Reinstatement in Fortune 500",
      "text": "In 2020, Intel was reinstated in the Fortune 500 list, ranking 45th and being the 7th-largest technology company in the ranking.",
