      "text": "The first Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade took place on November 27, 1924. It was organized by Macy's employees and featured floats, professional bands, and live animals borrowed from the Central Park Zoo.",
      "date": "1924-11-27"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Balloons",
      "text": "In 1927, the parade introduced its first giant balloons, replacing live animals. The first balloon was Felix the Cat.",
      "date": "1927"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Helium Shortage Affects Parade",
      "text": "In 1958, due to a helium shortage, the balloons were inflated with air and suspended from cranes instead of floating.",
      "date": "1958"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First National Television Broadcast",
      "text": "The Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade was first televised nationally on NBC in 1953, allowing viewers across the United States to watch the event.",
      "date": "1953"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Parade Cancelled During World War II",
      "text": "The parade was cancelled from 1942 to 1944 due to World War II, as rubber and helium were needed for the war effort.",
      "date": "1942"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Parade Resumes After World War II",
      "text": "The Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade resumed in 1945 after a three-year hiatus due to World War II.",
      "date": "1945"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Parade Broadcast in Color",
      "text": "In 1960, the parade was broadcast in color for the first time, enhancing the viewing experience for audiences.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of New Parade Route",
      "text": "In 2009, the parade route was changed to accommodate larger crowds and improve safety, starting at 77th Street and Central Park West and ending at Macy's Herald Square.",
      "date": "2009"
    }
  ]
}

