      "headline": "Chernobyl Disaster Occurs",
      "text": "On April 26, 1986, the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant in Ukraine experienced a catastrophic nuclear accident, leading to the release of large quantities of radioactive particles into the atmosphere.",
      "date": "1986-04-26"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Formation of the Elephant's Foot",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the Chernobyl disaster, a large mass of corium, later known as the Elephant's Foot, formed beneath Reactor 4. This mass was composed of molten concrete, sand, steel, uranium, and zirconium.",
      "date": "1986-04"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Elephant's Foot",
      "text": "The Elephant's Foot was discovered in December 1986 during an inspection of the reactor's basement. It was found to be extremely radioactive, emitting lethal doses of radiation.",
      "date": "1986-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Radiation Levels",
      "text": "When first discovered, the Elephant's Foot was emitting approximately 10,000 roentgens per hour, a level that could deliver a fatal dose of radiation in just 300 seconds.",
      "date": "1986-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Radiation Levels Decrease",
      "text": "Over time, the radiation levels emitted by the Elephant's Foot have decreased significantly due to the decay of radioactive isotopes.",
      "date": "1987"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Current Status",
      "text": "As of recent assessments, the Elephant's Foot remains highly radioactive but is no longer as lethal as it once was. It continues to be a subject of study for scientists monitoring the long-term effects of the Chernobyl disaster.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

