      "headline": "Birth of Cicero",
      "text": "Marcus Tullius Cicero was born on January 3, 106 BCE, in Arpinum, a town southeast of Rome.",
      "date": "-0106-01-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Education",
      "text": "Cicero was educated in Rome and later in Greece, where he studied philosophy and rhetoric, which laid the foundation for his future career as an orator and statesman.",
      "date": "-0086"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's First Major Legal Case",
      "text": "In 81 BCE, Cicero took on his first major legal case, defending Sextus Roscius on a charge of parricide, which established his reputation as a formidable orator.",
      "date": "-0081"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Quaestorship in Sicily",
      "text": "In 75 BCE, Cicero served as quaestor in Sicily, where he gained a reputation for integrity and fairness.",
      "date": "-0075"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Aedileship",
      "text": "Cicero was elected aedile in 69 BCE, a position that involved the maintenance of public buildings and the organization of public games.",
      "date": "-0069"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Praetorship",
      "text": "In 66 BCE, Cicero was elected praetor, a senior magistrate position, during which he supported the Lex Manilia, granting Pompey command in the war against Mithridates.",
      "date": "-0066"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Consulship",
      "text": "Cicero was elected consul in 63 BCE. During his consulship, he famously thwarted the Catiline Conspiracy, a plot to overthrow the Roman Republic.",
      "date": "-0063"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Exile from Rome",
      "text": "In 58 BCE, Cicero was exiled from Rome due to his execution of Catiline conspirators without trial, which was deemed illegal. He was recalled from exile in 57 BCE.",
      "date": "-0058"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Governorship in Cilicia",
      "text": "Cicero served as governor of Cilicia from 51 to 50 BCE, where he achieved some military success and was hailed as imperator by his troops.",
      "date": "-0051"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Return to Rome",
      "text": "Cicero returned to Rome in 50 BCE, just before the outbreak of the civil war between Julius Caesar and Pompey.",
      "date": "-0050"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Cicero's Philippics Against Mark Antony",
      "text": "In 44-43 BCE, Cicero delivered a series of speeches known as the Philippics, attacking Mark Antony and defending the Roman Republic.",
      "date": "-0044"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Cicero",
      "text": "Cicero was executed on December 7, 43 BCE, as part of the proscriptions ordered by the Second Triumvirate, which included Mark Antony.",
      "date": "-0043-12-07"
    }
  ]
}

