      "headline": "Tōhoku Earthquake Occurs",
      "text": "On 11 March 2011, at 14:46 JST, a magnitude 9.0–9.1 undersea megathrust earthquake occurred in the Pacific Ocean, 72 km east of the Oshika Peninsula of the Tōhoku region. The earthquake lasted approximately six minutes.",
      "date": "2011-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tsunami Strikes Japan",
      "text": "Following the earthquake on 11 March 2011, a massive tsunami was triggered, causing widespread devastation along the coast of northeastern Japan. The tsunami waves reached heights of up to 40.5 meters in Miyako, Iwate Prefecture, and traveled up to 10 km inland in Sendai.",
      "date": "2011-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Disaster",
      "text": "The tsunami caused a nuclear accident at the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant on 11 March 2011. The plant suffered meltdowns in three of its reactors, leading to the release of radioactive materials.",
      "date": "2011-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Japan Declares Nuclear Emergency",
      "text": "On 11 March 2011, the Japanese government declared a nuclear emergency following the Fukushima Daiichi nuclear disaster, and evacuations were ordered for residents within a 20 km radius of the plant.",
      "date": "2011-03-11"
    },
    {
      "headline": "International Aid and Response",
      "text": "In the days following the disaster, countries around the world offered aid and assistance to Japan. The United States, China, and many other nations sent rescue teams, supplies, and financial aid to help with relief efforts.",
      "date": "2011-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Aftershocks Continue",
      "text": "In the weeks and months following the initial earthquake, numerous aftershocks continued to affect the region, some of which were of significant magnitude, causing further damage and disruption.",
      "date": "2011-03"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Reconstruction Efforts Begin",
      "text": "In the aftermath of the disaster, Japan began extensive reconstruction efforts to rebuild the affected areas, restore infrastructure, and support displaced residents. These efforts have continued for years.",
      "date": "2011"
    }
  ]
}

