      "headline": "Death of Prasutagus",
      "text": "In AD 60, Prasutagus, the king of the Iceni and husband of Boudica, died. In his will, he left his kingdom jointly to his daughters and the Roman Emperor, hoping to secure protection for his family.",
      "date": "0060"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Roman Mistreatment and Rebellion",
      "text": "Following Prasutagus's death in AD 60, the Romans ignored his will, annexed his kingdom, and mistreated Boudica and her daughters. This led Boudica to lead a rebellion against Roman rule.",
      "date": "0060"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Boudica's Uprising",
      "text": "In AD 60 or 61, Boudica led a major uprising against the Roman Empire. Her forces destroyed Camulodunum (modern Colchester), Londinium (London), and Verulamium (St Albans), causing significant destruction.",
      "date": "0061"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Battle of Watling Street",
      "text": "In AD 61, Boudica's forces were decisively defeated by the Roman army led by Governor Gaius Suetonius Paulinus at the Battle of Watling Street. This marked the end of her rebellion.",
      "date": "0061"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Death of Boudica",
      "text": "Following her defeat, Boudica is believed to have died in AD 61, possibly by suicide to avoid capture by the Romans.",
