      "headline": "Inaugural International Men's Day",
      "text": "The first International Men's Day was inaugurated on February 7, 1992, by Thomas Oaster in Kansas City, Missouri, United States.",
      "date": "1992-02-07"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Revival of International Men's Day",
      "text": "In 1999, International Men's Day was revived by Dr. Jerome Teelucksingh from the University of the West Indies in Trinidad and Tobago. He chose November 19 to honor his father's birthday and to celebrate a day when a local soccer team had united the country.",
      "date": "1999-11-19"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Adoption of the Six Pillars",
      "text": "The objectives of International Men's Day were formalized into 'All the Six Pillars of International Men's Day', which include promoting positive male role models, celebrating men's positive contributions, focusing on men's health and well-being, highlighting discrimination against men, improving gender relations, and creating a safer, better world.",
      "date": "2009"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Global Recognition",
      "text": "By 2013, International Men's Day was celebrated in over 60 countries worldwide, gaining recognition and support from various organizations and individuals.",
      "date": "2013"
    },
    {
      "headline": "United Nations Acknowledgment",
      "text": "In 2015, the United Nations recognized International Men's Day, further solidifying its importance as a global awareness day.",
      "date": "2015"
    }
  ]
}

