      "headline": "Wall Collapses Near Holy Innocents' Cemetery",
      "text": "In 1774, a series of basement wall collapses around the Holy Innocents' Cemetery in Paris added urgency to the efforts to eliminate the city's overflowing cemeteries.",
      "date": "1774"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Preparation Work Begins",
      "text": "Shortly after the 1774 wall collapses, preparation work began to consolidate Paris's ancient stone quarries and create an ossuary to address the cemetery overflow issue.",
      "date": "1774"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Creation of the Ossuary",
      "text": "In 1786, the decision was made to transform the underground quarries into an ossuary, which would later become known as the Catacombs of Paris.",
      "date": "1786"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Transfer of Remains Begins",
      "text": "Starting in 1788, nightly processions of covered wagons began transferring remains from most of Paris's cemeteries to a mine shaft opened near the Rue de la Tombe-Issoire.",
      "date": "1788"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Catacombs Open to the Public",
      "text": "In 1809, the Catacombs of Paris were opened to the public, allowing visitors to explore the underground ossuaries.",
      "date": "1809"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Renovations and Improvements",
      "text": "Throughout the 19th century, various renovations and improvements were made to the Catacombs to enhance safety and accessibility for visitors.",
      "date": "1800s"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Modern-Day Tourist Attraction",
      "text": "Today, the Catacombs of Paris are a popular tourist attraction, drawing visitors from around the world to explore the historic ossuaries.",
      "date": "2023"
    }
  ]
}

