      "headline": "Sumerian Poems about Gilgamesh",
      "text": "The literary history of Gilgamesh begins with five Sumerian poems about Gilgamesh, king of Uruk. These poems may date back to the Third Dynasty of Ur, around the 21st century BCE.",
      "date": "-0200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Old Babylonian Version",
      "text": "The first surviving version of the combined epic, known as the 'Old Babylonian' version, dates back to the 18th century BCE. It is titled after its incipit, Shūtur eli sharrī. Only a few tablets of it have survived.",
      "date": "-01800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Standard Babylonian Version",
      "text": "The later Standard Babylonian version, compiled by Sîn-lēqi-unninni, dates to somewhere between the 13th to the 10th centuries BCE. It bears the incipit Sha naqba īmuru. Approximately two-thirds of this longer, twelve-tablet version have been recovered.",
      "date": "-01200"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery in Ashurbanipal's Library",
      "text": "Some of the best copies of the Epic of Gilgamesh were discovered in the library ruins of the 7th-century BCE Assyrian king Ashurbanipal.",
      "date": "-0700"
    }
  ]
}

