      "headline": "Formation of the Torah",
      "text": "The Torah, also known as the Pentateuch, is traditionally considered to have been written by Moses. Modern scholarship suggests that it was composed over several centuries, with the final form being completed around the 6th century BCE.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Composition of the Nevi'im",
      "text": "The Nevi'im, or Prophets, is the second section of the Hebrew Bible. It includes historical narratives and prophetic books, believed to have been composed between the 8th and 5th centuries BCE.",
      "date": "-0800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Compilation of the Ketuvim",
      "text": "The Ketuvim, or Writings, is the third section of the Hebrew Bible. It includes a diverse collection of texts, such as Psalms, Proverbs, and Job, compiled between the 6th and 4th centuries BCE.",
      "date": "-0600"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Translation of the Septuagint",
      "text": "The Septuagint, a Greek translation of the Hebrew Bible, was completed in the 3rd century BCE. It was widely used in the Hellenistic Jewish diaspora.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Dead Sea Scrolls",
      "text": "The Dead Sea Scrolls, ancient Jewish texts discovered in the Qumran Caves near the Dead Sea, include some of the oldest known manuscripts of the Hebrew Bible, dating from the 3rd century BCE to the 1st century CE.",
      "date": "-0300"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Masoretic Text",
      "text": "The Masoretic Text, the authoritative Hebrew text of the Jewish Bible, was compiled by the Masoretes between the 7th and 10th centuries CE. It is the basis for most modern editions of the Hebrew Bible.",
      "date": "0700"
    }
  ]
}

