      "text": "The first hamburger served on a bun is claimed to have been made by Charlie Nagreen in Seymour, Wisconsin, at the Outagamie County Fair in 1885. He flattened a meatball and placed it between two slices of bread.",
      "date": "1885"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Louis' Lunch",
      "text": "In 1900, Louis Lassen of Louis' Lunch in New Haven, Connecticut, is said to have served ground beef patties between slices of bread, which is considered one of the earliest forms of the hamburger.",
      "date": "1900"
    },
    {
      "headline": "White Castle Founded",
      "text": "White Castle, the first fast-food chain to sell hamburgers, was founded in Wichita, Kansas, in 1921. It played a significant role in popularizing the hamburger across the United States.",
      "date": "1921"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of the Cheeseburger",
      "text": "The cheeseburger was reportedly invented by Lionel Sternberger in Pasadena, California, in 1926 when he added a slice of American cheese to a hamburger.",
      "date": "1926"
    },
    {
      "headline": "McDonald's Opens",
      "text": "McDonald's, which would become one of the world's largest fast-food chains, opened its first restaurant in San Bernardino, California, in 1940, serving hamburgers as a staple menu item.",
      "date": "1940"
