      "headline": "Construction of the Antikythera Mechanism",
      "text": "The Antikythera mechanism was constructed in Ancient Greece, likely between 150 and 100 BCE. It is considered the oldest known example of an analogue computer.",
      "date": "-0150"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the Antikythera Mechanism",
      "text": "The Antikythera mechanism was discovered in 1901 by Greek sponge divers off the coast of the island of Antikythera, in a shipwreck dating from around 70–60 BCE.",
      "date": "1901"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Initial Study and Analysis",
      "text": "In 1902, the first analysis of the Antikythera mechanism was conducted by archaeologist Valerios Stais, who identified it as an astronomical clock.",
      "date": "1902"
    },
    {
      "headline": "X-ray Analysis",
      "text": "In 1971, Derek J. de Solla Price and Charalambos Karakalos conducted an X-ray analysis of the Antikythera mechanism, revealing its complex gear system.",
      "date": "1971"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Further Research and Understanding",
      "text": "In the early 21st century, advanced imaging techniques, including 3D X-ray tomography, were used to further study the Antikythera mechanism, leading to a better understanding of its functions and complexity.",
      "date": "2000"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Publication of New Findings",
      "text": "In 2006, a comprehensive study of the Antikythera mechanism was published, detailing its use in predicting astronomical positions and eclipses, as well as tracking the four-year cycle of athletic games.",
      "date": "2006"
    }
  ]
}

