      "headline": "Origins of the Yazidi Religion",
      "text": "The Yazidi religion is believed to have originated in the 12th century, combining elements of ancient Mesopotamian religions, Zoroastrianism, Islam, and Christianity.",
      "date": "1100"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir",
      "text": "Sheikh Adi ibn Musafir, a central figure in Yazidism, settled in the Lalish valley in the 12th century, where he established the Yazidi religious center.",
      "date": "1162"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Ottoman Empire Persecution",
      "text": "During the Ottoman Empire, Yazidis faced persecution and forced conversions, particularly in the 19th century.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Simele Massacre",
      "text": "In 1933, the Simele massacre occurred, where many Yazidis, along with Assyrians, were killed by the Iraqi army.",
      "date": "1933"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Saddam Hussein's Arabization Campaign",
      "text": "In the late 20th century, Saddam Hussein's regime conducted Arabization campaigns that displaced many Yazidis from their ancestral lands.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "2007 Yazidi Communities Bombings",
      "text": "On August 14, 2007, a series of coordinated bombings targeted Yazidi communities in Iraq, killing hundreds and injuring many more.",
      "date": "2007-08-14"
    },
    {
      "headline": "ISIS Genocide Against Yazidis",
      "text": "In August 2014, ISIS launched a campaign of genocide against the Yazidis in Sinjar, Iraq, resulting in mass killings, abductions, and displacement.",
      "date": "2014-08"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Recognition of Yazidi Genocide",
      "text": "In 2021, the United Nations recognized the atrocities committed by ISIS against the Yazidis as genocide.",
      "date": "2021"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
    {
      "headline": "First Description of Cystic Fibrosis",
      "text": "In 1938, cystic fibrosis was first described as a distinct disease by Dr. Dorothy Hansine Andersen. She identified it as a disorder of the pancreas and lungs.",
      "date": "1938"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of the CFTR Gene",
      "text": "In 1989, the gene responsible for cystic fibrosis, the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene, was discovered. This was a significant breakthrough in understanding the genetic basis of the disease.",
      "date": "1989"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of First CFTR Modulator",
      "text": "In 2012, the first CFTR modulator, Ivacaftor, was approved by the FDA. This drug targets the underlying cause of cystic fibrosis by improving the function of the defective CFTR protein.",
      "date": "2012"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of Combination Therapy",
      "text": "In 2015, the FDA approved a combination therapy of Ivacaftor and Lumacaftor, known as Orkambi, for treating cystic fibrosis patients with specific genetic mutations.",
      "date": "2015"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Approval of Triple Combination Therapy",
      "text": "In 2019, the FDA approved Trikafta, a triple combination therapy consisting of Elexacaftor, Tezacaftor, and Ivacaftor. This treatment significantly improved lung function in patients with the most common CF mutation.",
      "date": "2019"
    }
  ]
}

