      "text": "The Nebraska Territory was created by the Kansas-Nebraska Act on May 30, 1854. This act allowed the territories of Kansas and Nebraska to decide for themselves whether or not to allow slavery within their borders.",
      "date": "1854-05-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Nebraska Statehood",
      "text": "Nebraska was admitted to the Union as the 37th state on March 1, 1867, two years after the end of the American Civil War.",
      "date": "1867-03-01"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Unicameral Legislature Established",
      "text": "In 1937, Nebraska established a unicameral legislature, making it the only state in the United States with a single-house legislature. This change was implemented to increase efficiency and reduce costs.",
      "date": "1937"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Electoral College Vote Division",
      "text": "Nebraska, along with Maine, is one of only two states that divide their electoral college votes by congressional district rather than using a winner-take-all system. This system was adopted in 1992.",
      "date": "1992"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "timeline": [
