      "headline": "Loss of Pitch Control",
      "text": "On January 31, 2000, Alaska Airlines Flight 261 experienced a catastrophic loss of pitch control while flying over the Pacific Ocean.",
      "date": "2000-01-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Crash into Pacific Ocean",
      "text": "On January 31, 2000, Alaska Airlines Flight 261 crashed into the Pacific Ocean approximately 2.7 miles north of Anacapa Island, California. The crash resulted in the deaths of all 88 people on board, including 5 crew members and 83 passengers.",
      "date": "2000-01-31"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Investigation and Findings",
      "text": "The investigation into the crash of Alaska Airlines Flight 261 revealed that the probable cause was a loss of pitch control resulting from the in-flight failure of the horizontal stabilizer trim system jackscrew assembly's acme nut threads. This was due to insufficient lubrication of the jackscrew assembly.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

