      "headline": "First Recorded Outbreak in Europe",
      "text": "The first well-documented outbreak of what is now known as syphilis occurred in 1494-1495 among French troops besieging Naples in Italy. This outbreak is often cited as the beginning of the syphilis epidemic in Europe.",
      "date": "1494"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Naming of Syphilis",
      "text": "The name 'syphilis' was coined by the Italian physician and poet Girolamo Fracastoro in 1530 in his epic poem 'Syphilis sive morbus gallicus' (Syphilis or the French Disease).",
      "date": "1530"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Discovery of Treponema pallidum",
      "text": "In 1905, the bacterium Treponema pallidum, the causative agent of syphilis, was identified by Fritz Schaudinn and Erich Hoffmann.",
      "date": "1905"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Development of the Wassermann Test",
      "text": "In 1906, August von Wassermann developed the Wassermann test, the first blood test for syphilis, which greatly improved the ability to diagnose the disease.",
      "date": "1906"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Introduction of Penicillin as Treatment",
      "text": "In 1943, penicillin was first used to treat syphilis, revolutionizing the treatment of the disease and significantly reducing its prevalence.",
      "date": "1943"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Tuskegee Syphilis Study Ends",
      "text": "The infamous Tuskegee Syphilis Study, which began in 1932 and involved unethical experimentation on African American men, was officially ended in 1972 following public outcry.",
      "date": "1972"
    }
  ]
}

