    {
      "headline": "Ancient Greece and Rome",
      "text": "In ancient Greece and Rome, bisexuality was a common and accepted part of society. Relationships between men and women, as well as same-sex relationships, were documented and often celebrated in literature and art.",
      "date": "-0500"
    },
    {
      "headline": "19th Century: Emergence of the Term",
      "text": "The term 'bisexuality' began to emerge in the 19th century, initially used in a biological context to describe organisms with both male and female reproductive organs. It later evolved to describe sexual attraction to both men and women.",
      "date": "1800"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1948: Kinsey Report",
      "text": "In 1948, Alfred Kinsey published 'Sexual Behavior in the Human Male', which introduced the Kinsey Scale. This scale suggested that sexual orientation exists on a continuum, challenging the binary view of heterosexuality and homosexuality and acknowledging bisexuality.",
      "date": "1948"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1970s: Bisexual Activism",
      "text": "The 1970s saw the rise of bisexual activism, with the formation of the first bisexual groups and the organization of events to raise awareness and promote bisexual rights.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1990: Bisexuality in the DSM",
      "text": "In 1990, the American Psychiatric Association removed homosexuality from the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM), which also impacted the perception and acceptance of bisexuality.",
      "date": "1990"
    },
    {
      "headline": "1999: Celebrate Bisexuality Day",
      "text": "Celebrate Bisexuality Day was first observed on September 23, 1999. It was created to recognize and celebrate bisexual people, their history, and their community.",
      "date": "1999-09-23"
    },
    {
      "headline": "21st Century: Increased Visibility",
      "text": "In the 21st century, bisexuality has gained increased visibility and acceptance, with more representation in media, literature, and public discourse.",
      "date": "2000"
    }
  ]
}

