      "text": "The European Parliament was established in 1952 as the Common Assembly of the European Coal and Steel Community (ECSC).",
      "date": "1952"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Direct Elections",
      "text": "The first direct elections to the European Parliament were held in June 1979, allowing citizens of the European Community to directly elect Members of the European Parliament (MEPs).",
      "date": "1979-06"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Single European Act",
      "text": "The Single European Act, which came into effect in 1987, increased the powers of the European Parliament, particularly in the legislative process.",
      "date": "1987"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Maastricht Treaty",
      "text": "The Maastricht Treaty, which came into force in 1993, further expanded the powers of the European Parliament, introducing the co-decision procedure, which gave the Parliament equal legislative power with the Council in certain areas.",
      "date": "1993"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Amsterdam Treaty",
      "text": "The Amsterdam Treaty, effective from 1999, extended the co-decision procedure to more areas, thus increasing the legislative power of the European Parliament.",
      "date": "1999"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Lisbon Treaty",
      "text": "The Lisbon Treaty, which came into force in December 2009, significantly increased the powers of the European Parliament, making it a co-legislator with the Council of the European Union in almost all areas of EU legislation.",
      "date": "2009-12"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Brexit and Reduction of MEPs",
      "text": "Following Brexit, the number of MEPs was reduced from 751 to 705 in 2020.",
      "date": "2020"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Increase in MEPs",
      "text": "After the June 2024 European elections, the number of MEPs increased from 705 to 720.",
      "date": "2024-06"
    }
