      "headline": "Birth of Marina Abramović",
      "text": "Marina Abramović was born on November 30, 1946, in Belgrade, Serbia, then part of Yugoslavia.",
      "date": "1946-11-30"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Early Education and Influences",
      "text": "In the early 1970s, Abramović studied at the Academy of Fine Arts in Belgrade and later completed postgraduate studies at the Academy of Fine Arts in Zagreb, Croatia.",
      "date": "1970"
    },
    {
      "headline": "First Performance Artworks",
      "text": "In 1973, Abramović began creating performance art pieces, starting with 'Rhythm 10', which involved the use of knives and was performed in Edinburgh.",
      "date": "1973"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rhythm Series",
      "text": "Between 1973 and 1974, Abramović performed a series of works known as the 'Rhythm' series, including 'Rhythm 0' in 1974, where she allowed the audience to use objects on her body.",
      "date": "1974"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Collaboration with Ulay",
      "text": "In 1976, Abramović began a collaboration with the German artist Ulay (Uwe Laysiepen), which lasted until 1988. They created numerous performances together, exploring themes of duality and identity.",
      "date": "1976"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Lovers: The Great Wall Walk",
      "text": "In 1988, Abramović and Ulay ended their collaboration with a performance called 'The Lovers', where they walked from opposite ends of the Great Wall of China and met in the middle to say goodbye.",
      "date": "1988"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Solo Career and Major Works",
      "text": "After separating from Ulay, Abramović continued her solo career, creating significant works such as 'Balkan Baroque' in 1997, which won the Golden Lion at the Venice Biennale.",
      "date": "1997"
    },
    {
      "headline": "The Artist is Present",
      "text": "In 2010, Abramović performed 'The Artist is Present' at the Museum of Modern Art in New York, where she sat silently across from visitors for 736 hours and 30 minutes.",
      "date": "2010"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Founding of Marina Abramović Institute",
      "text": "In 2007, Abramović founded the Marina Abramović Institute (MAI), a non-profit foundation for performance art.",
      "date": "2007"
