      "text": "In 1930, the Motion Picture Production Code, also known as the Hays Code, was established in the United States. It imposed strict guidelines on the portrayal of sex and other moral issues in films, effectively restricting unsimulated sex scenes in mainstream cinema.",
      "date": "1930"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Decriminalization of Sexually Explicit Films",
      "text": "During the 1960s, social attitudes towards sex began to shift, leading to the decriminalization of sexually explicit films in many countries. This change allowed for more freedom in the depiction of sexual content in films.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Rise of Underground Films",
      "text": "Before the decriminalization of explicit content, films showing unsimulated sex were confined to privately distributed underground films, such as stag films or 'porn loops'. These films were not subject to the same restrictions as mainstream cinema.",
      "date": "1960"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Mainstream Films with Unsimulated Sex",
      "text": "In the years following the 1960s, a few mainstream films began to include unsimulated sex scenes, although it remained uncommon. These films often sparked controversy and debate about the boundaries of art and pornography.",
      "date": "1970"
    }
  ]
}

